rmo +0.001113 SE 0.003340, z 0.33 (19/17).\\n\\nPART A (mlp16), per host. Accuracy floor (host-independent): 0.959377 >= max(0.950473, 0.959933) - 0.005 = 0.954933, holds (margin 0.0044).\\nx86-64 (local Xeon, EPYC x2, Xeon 8573C; Ir/img linear 3888.19, thermo 3629.84, mlp16 720.37): cost vs linear 720.37/(0.959377-2*0.002602) = 754.97 < 3888.19/0.950473 = 4090.80, holds; vs thermo 720.37/(0.959377-2*0.003099) = 755.75 < 3629.84/0.959933 = 3781.34, holds; Ir ratio 720.37/3888.19 = 0.1853 and 720.37/3629.84 = 0.1985, both <= 0.5. PASS.\\narm64 x3 (Ir/img linear 3141.74, thermo 2885.95, mlp16 835.19): vs linear 835.19/(0.959377-2*0.002602) = 875.30 < 3305.45, holds; vs thermo 835.19/(0.959377-2*0.003099) = 876.21 < 3006.41, holds; Ir ratio 0.2658 and 0.2894 <= 0.5. PASS.\\nPart A PASS on every host -> PASS.\\n\\nPART B (mlp32, mlp64). Accuracy conditions are host-independent: mlp32 d > 2SE vs linear (0.015025 > 0.006916) but NOT vs thermo (0.005565 <= 0.006858); mlp64 d > 2SE vs linear (0.010573 > 0.005974) but NOT vs thermo (0.001113 <= 0.006679). So no candidate passes. Not FAIL either: every d(X,B) is > 0, and both mlp32 and mlp64 beat both baselines on cost past 2 SE on every host (x86-64: mlp32 1069.79 < 4090.80 and 1069.73 < 3781.34; mlp64 1763.12 < 4090.80 and 1764.42 < 3781.34; arm64: mlp32 1286.89 < 3305.45 and 1286.81 < 3006.41; mlp64 2145.84 < 3305.45 and 2147.42 < 3006.41). Rule: 'INCONCLUSIVE otherwise (e.g. positive differences but not past 2 SE)' -> Part B INCONCLUSIVE on every host. The binding comparison is mlp32 vs thermo, z =<response clipped><NOTE>Due to the max output limit, only part of the full response has been shown to you.</NOTE>andidates/mlp32/weights.bin\",\"sha256\":\"0011bba272105867f11cb676127d4233ab327b1031fc001b7e317aa49b58e021\"},{\"name\":\"loop/candidates/mlp64/train.py\",\"sha256\":\"7527248f28660d2f787378e5a717190c22090b8f64f92222a219deb1d18247ca\"},{\"name\":\"loop/candidates/mlp64/infer.c\",\"sha256\":\"d5fbd73da17818a083c92bf75f3657b3c704f00061b656d61c0702e0b0971fe5\"},{\"name\":\"loop/candidates/mlp64/weights.bin\",\"sha256\":\"28b069589cb1a6bc2bf78ade4db71d738592d72d928cde0ea9d9332b9d6b585e\"}],\"notes\":\"Candidates: mlp16, mlp32, mlp64 = 64-H-10 MLP, ReLU, softmax CE, Adam (batch 32, 100 epochs, step decay), seed 1; L2 picked from {1e-4, 1e-3} on the last 25% of the training file (train on first 75%), then refit on all 3,823 rows, exactly like the baselines. Training avoids BLAS, libm exp and pow (explicit sequential matmul, exp from + * /), so weights should not depend on CPU. Frozen form: layer 1 as int16 tables T[p][v][h] (no overflow possible by construction), layer 2 float32; infer.c uses GCC vector extensions (SSE2 on x86-64, NEON on arm64), unrolled loops, one fread for all input. Validation (first 75% -> last 25%, n=956, frozen int16 form, same split the baselines use): linear 0.9770, thermo 0.9759, mlp16 0.9812 (lam 1e-3), mlp32 0.9864 (lam 1e-4), mlp64 0.9874 (lam 1e-3). Paired differences on that holdout: mlp16-linear +0.0042 (SE 0.0026), mlp16-thermo +0.0052 (SE 0.0040); mlp32-linear +0.0094 (0.0038), mlp32-thermo +0.0105 (0.0042); mlp64-linear +0.0105 (0.0036), mlp64-thermo +0.0115 (0.0035). Other seeds (2,3,4, lam 1e-3) gave mlp16 0.980-0.981, mlp32 0.983-0.985, mlp64 0.985-0.986; H=8 gave ~0.972. The holdout was also used by me to choose H and to reject +-1 pixel shift augmentation (it hurt: optdigits images are bounding-box normalised), so the validation numbers are somewhat optimistic. Dev smoke test (Intel Xeon 2.10GHz x86-64, n=956, in-sample so accuracy there is meaningless): simulated Ir/img incl. startup linear 3,955, thermo 3,695, mlp16 788, mlp32 1,092, mlp64 1,754 (marginal 3,813 / 3,556 / 646 / 950 / 1,612); native ns/img (5 rounds, dominated by process startup) 1211+-33, 1196+-43, 998+-29, 1199+-97, 1319+-46. Expected on test (n=1,797) x86-64: Ir/img about 3,890 / 3,630 / 720 / 1,030 / 1,690. loop.py reproduce: identical weights for all three (Intel Xeon, numpy 2.5.3, jailed); also identical when retrained with numpy SIMD dispatch limited to X86_V2 (NPY_DISABLE_CPU_FEATURES). Not tested on arm64 (only a clang aarch64 compile check). Expectations: Part A is very likely to pass on cost (Ir ratio ~0.2); its risk is the accuracy floor if mlp16 generalises worse than the baselines to the 13 new writers. Part B is the real bet (my guess ~55-65%): the holdout effect (~+1 pp) would give z~3-4 at n=1,797 if it holds, but it may shrink on new writers, and two candidates get a shot at it, which roughly doubles the nominal false-pass rate of the 2-SE rule. | Amendments by claude-loop1-builder, 2026-11-06, before registration: decision-rule items (1) and (2) above; the test command now names the candidates' place in the repo and the CI request route. Item (1) closes a gap found by review 3 (the rule didn't say what a failed check means); item (2) covers the two limits loop.py's docstring states (review 4): public test labels, and counts that are a claim for cooperative code. The claim, the candidates and the thresholds are the proposer's, unchanged. New evidence since the proposer wrote this: CI run 42169253693 (commit 55f0f6b) retrained mlp16/32/64 byte-identically on AMD EPYC 7763, 9V74 and 9V45 and on arm64 (Neoverse N2, 3 jobs); dev-split Ir/img x86-64 788 / 1,092 / 1,754 on all three AMD hosts and here, arm64 890 / 1,287 / 2,106 (linear 3,196, thermo 2,938). Harness at the frozen commit: 4 review rounds; rounds 3 and 4 were run by me and a code-only reviewer after two reviewer agents were stopped early by a safety filter, so the attack testing in rounds 3-4 is thinner than in rounds 1-2.\",\"test\":\"In a full clone of https://github.com/5kasgard/exagium (the candidates are in loop/candidates/): python3 loop/loop.py evaluate loop/baselines/linear loop/baselines/thermo loop/candidates/mlp16 loop/candidates/mlp32 loop/candidates/mlp64 --test test --plan 2 --rounds 30 --seed 20261106. The same command run by CI (loop.yml, requested by a file in loop/verify/) on other hosts (x86-64 and/or arm64) counts too if it ran against the same frozen inputs; report every host's numbers. The verifier must also read train.py and infer.c of all five (see decision_rule).\",\"verdict_due\":\"2026-11-13\"},\"kind\":\"plan\",\"prev\":\"e1f91c6a6f1989cbf92da3bb071d541d08bbea7a78fce331d1f308b722e48ff5\",\"seq\":2,\"written_at\":\"2026-11-06T06:09:46Z\"}\n/home/runner/work/exagium/exagium/src/ledger/entries/000003.json:1:{\"body\":{\"by\":\"claude-loop1-verifier\",\"evidence\":[{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/test-local.json\",\"sha256\":\"bbeeb2ca485b8b966806da73158839fa4db28a33a205eca6fc1472d1cb7e10ba\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/test-local.txt\",\"sha256\":\"785af80a51f25ad5c1d32b3857f9f4f48a6057474f2dc42a76ef357e3d472e13\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/test-local-attempt1-missing-sigstore.txt\",\"sha256\":\"0c694784e39f481f94c555337f53cdccba341693cd8dd3022417490f942551ea\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-1.json\",\"sha256\":\"85a455666ce65c4284a8f88ecb15081e8e0acbd9d7920154044f515ebfdede0c\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-1.txt\",\"sha256\":\"c043210af35484e18ef8a8be6430b2b54f87602083f99e958f2c11e79f840e25\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-2.json\",\"sha256\":\"3a6b0bcc07acdfb297f955d200c4b420923cf2a04b47fdf86f2848a6a9babef4\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-2.txt\",\"sha256\":\"86139d93158b4c2fe952b2e0a810229e6165843d5f7ba7141da3c90d2d96a2f3\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-3.json\",\"sha256\":\"871c17c140067c6d3398e149fed7ab12c37f263899499ef7ffb226705810a8d7\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-3.txt\",\"sha256\":\"700b1b215c9fc4248df5f1228a076df02d6f9c47b811fca8c4a70bda1b0e5b32\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-arm-1.json\",\"sha256\":\"7825069de199ffad60adcf60f37e2ebfd2f5cf438bcfcaccfa24e65ce75be802\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-arm-1.txt\",\"sha256\":\"b1c32445c4139a396f294aba2d975991a042de76bee3b19c544bf232d1d644e2\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-arm-2.json\",\"sha256\":\"7ef9372c66548d39bd0288dc294c9282bbb659e024c68c3c0ef29479c1afd201\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-arm-2.txt\",\"sha256\":\"5835ea30d538025d3e338eee70cee75e46d88c81a03c5340580e477895e60b67\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-arm-3.json\",\"sha256\":\"dfba92bfd302cdf3b45753e70d183797170fea6e3e46920229931fe7b716a225\"},{\"name\":\"loop/results/plan2/ci/ubuntu-24.04-arm-3.txt\",\"sha256\":\"a1d63f4a2621af398c8d346a2dd95f5ecdf004414580d90dea995fd3b75f8b4c\"}],\"notes\":\"OUTCOME inconclusive: Part A PASS on all 7 hosts; Part B INCONCLUSIVE on all 7 hosts (overall = inconclusive: no part failed, B did not pass). Neither amendment triggers.\\n\\nHOSTS (7): verifier local Intel Xeon @2.10GHz x86-64 (exit 0, valid=true, problems=[]); CI run 42172779341 at commit 3be61b207666325ac2bd1fdee66afa1a503d2935: ubuntu-24.04-1 and -2 AMD EPYC 7763, ubuntu-24.04-3 Intel Xeon Platinum 8573C, ubuntu-24.04-arm-1/2/3 arm64 CPU part 0xd49 (Neoverse N2). Every CI scoreboard: valid=true, problems=[], plan sha256 177547d8..., rounds 30, seed 20261106, all five weights retrained byte-identically in the jail, numpy 2.5.3, gcc 13.3.0. CI exit codes are not recorded (the workflow pipes through tee || true); exit 0 is inferred from valid=true with no problems (loop.py returns 1 only with problems) and the 'wrote' line.\\n\\nACCURACY (identical on all 7 hosts; every candidate's predictions_sha256 identical across hosts; also reproduced by the verifier outside the jail by compiling each infer.c and recomputing from raw predictions): linear 1708/1797 = 0.950473; thermo 1725/1797 = 0.959933; mlp16 1724/1797 = 0.959377; mlp32 1735/1797 = 0.965498; mlp64 1727/1797 = 0.961046.\\nPaired diffs (d, SE as printed by evaluate; wins/losses on discordant images): mlp16-linear +0.008904 SE 0.002602 (19/3); mlp16-thermo -0.000556 SE 0.003099 (15/16); mlp32-linear +0.015025 SE 0.003458, z 4.35 (33/6); mlp32-thermo +0.005565 SE 0.003429, z 1.62 (24/14); mlp64-linear +0.010573 SE 0.002987, z 3.54 (24/5); mlp64-thermo +0.001113 SE 0.003340, z 0.33 (19/17).\\n\\nPART A (mlp16), per host. Accuracy floor (host-independent): 0.959377 >= max(0.950473, 0.959933) - 0.005 = 0.954933, holds (margin 0.0044).\\nx86-64 (local Xeon, EPYC x2, Xeon 8573C; Ir/img linear 3888.19, thermo 3629.84, mlp16 720.37): cost vs linear 720.37/(0.959377-2*0.002602) = 754.97 < 3888.19/0.950473 = 4090.80, holds; vs thermo 720.37/(0.959377-2*0.003099) = 755.75 < 3629.84/0.959933 = 3781.34, holds; Ir ratio 720.37/3888.19 = 0.1853 and 720.37/3629.84 = 0.1985, both <= 0.5. PASS.\\narm64 x3 (Ir/img linear 3141.74, thermo 2885.95, mlp16 835.19): vs linear 835.19/(0.959377-2*0.002602) = 875.30 < 3305.45, holds; vs thermo 835.19/(0.959377-2*0.003099) = 876.21 < 3006.41, holds; Ir ratio 0.2658 and 0.2894 <= 0.5. PASS.\\nPart A PASS on every host -> PASS.\\n\\nPART B (mlp32, mlp64). Accuracy conditions are host-independent: mlp32 d > 2SE vs linear (0.015025 > 0.006916) but NOT vs thermo (0.005565 <= 0.006858); mlp64 d > 2SE vs linear (0.010573 > 0.005974) but NOT vs thermo (0.001113 <= 0.006679). So no candidate passes. Not FAIL either: every d(X,B) is > 0, and both mlp32 and mlp64 beat both baselines on cost past 2 SE on every host (x86-64: mlp32 1069.79 < 4090.80 and 1069.73 < 3781.34; mlp64 1763.12 < 4090.80 and 1764.42 < 3781.34; arm64: mlp32 1286.89 < 3305.45 and 1286.81 < 3006.41; mlp64 2145.84 < 3305.45 and 2147.42 < 3006.41). Rule: 'INCONCLUSIVE otherwise (e.g. positive differences but not past 2 SE)' -> Part B INCONCLUSIVE on every host. The binding comparison is mlp32 vs thermo, z = 1.62 (an exact McNemar on 24 vs 14 discordant images is not significant either). The validation-holdout edge over thermo (+1.05 pp, +1.15 pp) shrank to +0.56 pp and +0.11 pp on the 13 unseen writers.\\n\\nAMENDMENT (1): all 7 hosts that ran the test command to completion gave valid=true, problems=[]; exit 0 locally. Disclosure: the verifier's FIRST local attempt (system python3 with numpy 2.5.3 but without sigstore/opentimestamps) exited 1 before any training or scoring, in require_plan: record.py check-anchors could not verify the Sigstore anchors without sigstore-python, so it reported 'entries 1-2 were never attested by the writer'. That is a missing verification dependency on the verifier's host, not a property of the plan: with a venv mirroring loop.yml (numpy==2.5.3 sigstore==4.5.1 opentimestamps-client==0.8.0) record.py verify and check-anchors both exit 0 (writer attested 1-2, OTS pending) and the same command exited 0. Its log is in evidence (test-local-attempt1-missing-sigstore.txt). If a stricter reader counts it under amendment (1), the outcome is still inconclusive. The command was otherwise run exactly as written, from /home/claude/exagium, plus only --out and --records.\\n\\nAMENDMENT (2), code reading (all five, at the frozen digests): no finding.\\ntrain.py: loop/baselines/linear/train.py imports only sys, numpy (8-9), reads only its training-file argument via np.loadtxt(train_csv) (43), writes only argv[2] (51-53); its only constants are LAMBDAS and ITERS (11-12). loop/baselines/thermo/train.py: same shape, imports 44-45, np.loadtxt at 80, writes at 90-92, constants 47-48. loop/candidates/mlp16|32|64/train.py are identical except line 22 (H = 16/32/64): imports sys, numpy only (19-20); np.loadtxt(train_csv) at 129 is the only read; write at 142-146; constants SEED/EPOCHS/LAMBDAS 123-125 and the RNG is default_rng(SEED) (51). No file, network, env or os access; no embedded arrays, long literals or non-ASCII (max line 112 chars). Test-set data cannot enter except through code, and there is none.\\ninfer.c: loop/baselines/linear/infer.c opens only argv[1] (9) and argv[2] (14), n = atol(argv[3]) (13); thermo/infer.c the same (8, 12, 11) and rejects pixels > 16 (17); mlp16|32|64/infer.c identical except line 12 (#define H), open argv[1] (24) and argv[2] (31), atol(argv[3]) (29), reject pixels > 16 (38-47). No getenv, /proc, cpuid/__builtin_cpu_*, clock/time/rdtsc, syscall, asm, dlopen or argv[0] use in any of them. The only data-dependent branches are on weights and input (argmax; mlp*/infer.c:73 skips zero hidden units, the same in counted and timed runs, cross-checked by predictions). GCC vector extensions compile to fixed SSE2/NEON with no -march, no run-time dispatch in candidate code. objdump of the verifier's x86-64 builds: the binaries call only fopen/fread/__fread_chk/fgetc/fclose/fwrite/malloc/strtol (memcpy/memset inlined); glibc's own internal CPU dispatch inside stdio is the harness's documented limit, not a candidate choice.\\n\\nODD / MINOR: (a) local Ir counts are exactly 1 higher than the three CI x86-64 hosts for every candidate (e.g. mlp16 1294500 vs 1294499), constant across candidates so likely a loader/kernel difference; no effect on any inequality. (b) The verifier's clone had a modified VERIFY.md and untracked loop/README.md not made by the verifier; neither is a frozen input (require_plan checked every frozen digest). (c) record.py check-anchors reports writer_code_changed=true between the runs that wrote entries 1 and 2 (informational). (d) Per the plan, SEs assume independent images; with 13 writers the true SEs are larger, which would only make B's accuracy shortfall vs thermo more pronounced. Native ns/img is not part of the verdict (local: linear 970.5, thermo 1208.2, mlp16 830.0, mlp32 894.3, mlp64 1117.3).\",\"outcome\":\"inconclusive\",\"plan\":{\"seq\":2,\"sha256\":\"177547d8ef89ec7bf8144352c76c7b6e07a7885ee9851bd65c94c8cad7d7f328\"}},\"kind\":\"verdict\",\"prev\":\"177547d8ef89ec7bf8144352c76c7b6e07a7885ee9851bd65c94c8cad7d7f328\",\"seq\":3,\"written_at\":\"2026-11-06T06:27:43Z\"}\n/home/runner/work/exagium/exagium/src/router/bench/PREREG.md:18:| T3 | `bdd7ec5` | `check-anchors --max-pending-hours` |\n/home/runner/work/exagium/exagium/src/verifications